{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2282 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "488dc473342c6d813552325d56613279cc04e6903799303d37eed445bedf0274" :edb/db-before-t 2281 :edb/db-after-t 2282 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535594 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:10:40.468Z" 13194139535594 true] [17592186083147 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356710 13194139535594 true] [17592186083217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356697 13194139535594 true] [17592186083217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356713 13194139535594 true] [17592186083221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356704 13194139535594 true] [17592186083221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356720 13194139535594 true] [17592186356697 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356698 13194139535594 true] [17592186356697 1089 "q-112873" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356697 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535594 true] [17592186356697 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-4x+5?$" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356697 1113 #uuid "d8ff66df-4977-5b8b-890c-17e01de26de7" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356697 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $5$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }5}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{5}{6}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(6)$ is not a factor of $8.$" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356699 13194139535594 true] [17592186356698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356700 13194139535594 true] [17592186356698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356701 13194139535594 true] [17592186356698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356702 13194139535594 true] [17592186356698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356703 13194139535594 true] [17592186356698 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535594 true] [17592186356698 1041 "selection" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356698 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356699 13194139535594 true] [17592186356698 1110 #uuid "8077c67b-86a5-5d48-8b0c-7df2ce37e9c0" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356699 1034 "\\frac{5}{6}" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356699 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356699 1111 #uuid "6780c823-b306-56d1-8eff-1a05bbae2d2b" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356700 1034 "-\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356700 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356700 1111 #uuid "bbcdf7c6-1596-591f-99dc-3c3d302885e2" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356701 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356701 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356701 1111 #uuid "88a0b791-070a-5678-9cc3-7cbca1836c02" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356702 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356702 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356702 1111 #uuid "9518a177-d2d4-511e-8aaa-c5995d850ce7" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356703 1034 "\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356703 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356703 1111 #uuid "4fb5649c-8f2d-5678-b6a1-730ea192e41a" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356704 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356705 13194139535594 true] [17592186356704 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356708 13194139535594 true] [17592186356704 1089 "q-275954" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356704 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535594 true] [17592186356704 1100 "The polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+20x-12$ has a root in the interval $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2}).$ In **ascending order**, the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356704 1113 #uuid "297bc86c-010a-5357-8616-cf63e473194e" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356704 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-12$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-12}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2})$ are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $20$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-12$ | $12$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-8$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-8x+8).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-8x+8) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =(2x-3){(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$  and $x=2.$" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356706 13194139535594 true] [17592186356705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356707 13194139535594 true] [17592186356705 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535594 true] [17592186356705 1041 "field-1" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356705 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356707 13194139535594 true] [17592186356705 1110 #uuid "05b7a21e-a3c4-5d87-83c6-215690847613" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356706 1034 "1" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356706 1111 #uuid "95cc84a3-7d3d-58d3-9e86-4450e6289ea8" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356707 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356707 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356707 1111 #uuid "966d87a8-987b-57c0-a31e-38d67094453e" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356708 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356709 13194139535594 true] [17592186356708 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535594 true] [17592186356708 1041 "field-2" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356708 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356709 13194139535594 true] [17592186356708 1110 #uuid "1eab6f8c-fb5f-5d7a-80b3-7c3f501f2048" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356709 1034 "2" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356709 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356709 1111 #uuid "ca7fa490-f5fc-590b-ba89-6f0c2f7ed8c8" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356710 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356711 13194139535594 true] [17592186356710 1089 "q-321645" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356710 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535594 true] [17592186356710 1100 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3x+2$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139535594 true] [17592186356710 1113 #uuid "a8aff0b4-015d-5c83-900b-94f887fc4f2e" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356710 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 4$, and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ } \\\\ \\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $10$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 5=10.$)" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356711 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356712 13194139535594 true] [17592186356711 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535594 true] [17592186356711 1041 "field-1" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356711 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356712 13194139535594 true] [17592186356711 1110 #uuid "8f6b2ace-220d-5e68-a4e6-8b7ef7e7f437" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356712 1034 "10" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356712 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356712 1111 #uuid "dafd0e75-de9a-5941-9c87-35225447c01d" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356713 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356714 13194139535594 true] [17592186356713 1089 "q-6657" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356713 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535594 true] [17592186356713 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x-1?$" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356713 1113 #uuid "032195d0-c32a-510e-9d2d-9923dbb6a6bb" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356713 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-1$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-1}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-1.$" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356715 13194139535594 true] [17592186356714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356716 13194139535594 true] [17592186356714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356717 13194139535594 true] [17592186356714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356718 13194139535594 true] [17592186356714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356719 13194139535594 true] [17592186356714 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535594 true] [17592186356714 1041 "selection" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356714 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356717 13194139535594 true] [17592186356714 1110 #uuid "1d93d277-feb3-5525-9d4c-d8d49ee68342" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356715 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356715 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356715 1111 #uuid "163457f2-6548-5a9a-9b3f-6b7827f7da78" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356716 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356716 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356716 1111 #uuid "c1f26617-4b91-5b60-b50a-e30200dfaf9c" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356717 1034 "2" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356717 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356717 1111 #uuid "07056a20-6b0b-5f6d-b143-01a25742c779" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356718 1034 "1" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356718 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356718 1111 #uuid "e29ffd5e-fee2-5cf2-8700-7d068f661ef1" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356719 1034 "-1" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356719 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356719 1111 #uuid "081f460e-e19c-5d73-9189-e2138e267107" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356720 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356721 13194139535594 true] [17592186356720 1089 "q-83864" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356720 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535594 true] [17592186356720 1100 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-14x-24$ has one of its roots in the interval $(3,8),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356720 1113 #uuid "fb97b373-7d53-58da-98de-e4426085b429" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356720 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-24$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-24}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 12,\\,\\pm 24\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(3,8),$ are $4$ and $6.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-14$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $20$ | $24$ |\n|  | $1$ | $5$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+5x+6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-4)({x}^{2}+5x+6) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+2)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=4,-2,-3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n4-2-3=-1.\n$$" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356721 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356722 13194139535594 true] [17592186356721 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356723 13194139535594 true] [17592186356721 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356724 13194139535594 true] [17592186356721 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356725 13194139535594 true] [17592186356721 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356726 13194139535594 true] [17592186356721 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535594 true] [17592186356721 1041 "selection" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356721 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356725 13194139535594 true] [17592186356721 1110 #uuid "b62557ab-2302-5fb0-aee8-07f656d07c3f" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356722 1034 "1" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356722 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356722 1111 #uuid "faf4592b-d3e1-5234-bdee-38ed2d6320ec" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356723 1034 "-3" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356723 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356723 1111 #uuid "54528da9-e463-5820-92f7-d6e4ae089aaa" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356724 1034 "4" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356724 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356724 1111 #uuid "09db3249-ee7b-5058-819d-4e53cbd0fb6f" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356725 1034 "-1" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356725 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356725 1111 #uuid "2c4092e3-bf38-5bd0-89e0-61df52567a49" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356726 1034 "-2" 13194139535594 true] [17592186356726 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535594 true] [17592186356726 1111 #uuid "de497e44-7ceb-5c84-aa9f-baa25d63cc83" 13194139535594 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-112873" 17592186356697 "q-112873/selection" 17592186356698 "q-112873/selection/21bc1f5ac2b6db8ca11973d9b85c1eeb6af2cd920b556e0cf3d98ab9a728286a" 17592186356699 "q-112873/selection/35f5572ccca3a8c38d3ca06e2a92b51065f48e45d29833e19f4eb153cae096fc" 17592186356700 "q-112873/s